  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nConfidence is high that this heat wave will continue into the\nweekend. Ensembles demonstrate strong agreement in building the\naforementioned ridge back to the west Saturday into Sunday,\nbecoming centered over the Plains. Southwest Missouri and\nsoutheast Kansas will remain under its influence, and afternoon\nheat index values will continue to climb into the 100 to 110\ndegree range. Therefore, the Heat Advisory has been extended\nthrough 8 PM Sunday areawide. With some localized areas reaching\na 105+ heat index for the last two days, it is entirely possible\nthe four convective day criteria comes into play and an upgrade\nto an Extreme Heat Warning is needed. However, because the \nprevious observations of 105+ deg heat index and the forecast \nlocations of future 105+ deg heat index are not spatially \nconsistent with one another, confidence was not high enough to\nissue a warning for any specific location. \n\nAs the ridge builds across the Plains, a diffuse cold front \nwill sag southwestward into Missouri. Global models continue to\nvary in this front's progression, but its passage may bring\nslightly cooler temperatures to the eastern Ozarks and south-\ncentral Missouri early in the week. A few global models still\nwant to spit out isolated patches of light QPF, but these\nchances remain very low (10-15%) as the better upper-level energy\nremains displaced to the north and east.\n\nEnsemble clusters diverge more significantly by the middle of\nnext week. There is some signal, at least, of a flattening \nridge and an open door for rain chances. NBM PoPs are currently \nat 15-25% on Thursday. With the persistent heat and a lack of \nappreciable rainfall in the meantime, drought conditions will \ndeteriorate, and fuels will be more susceptible to fire starts. \nThese conditions will need to be monitored closely in the coming\nweeks for the potential of worsening impacts.",
